# Wildcard store subdomains — <slug>.tilla.gudman.xyz
#
# STATUS: the APP SIDE IS BUILT, TESTED AND DEPLOYED. The DNS record and the
# certificate below are NOT done and are owner-gated — see "What is blocked".
# Until they exist, nothing regresses: the subdomains simply do not resolve, and
# every store stays reachable at https://tilla.gudman.xyz/s/<slug>/ as before.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why bother
#
# `tilla.gudman.xyz/s/highland-roast/` reads as an entry in somebody else's
# directory. `highland-roast.tilla.gudman.xyz` reads as the merchant's own shop.
# Same store, same rails; the difference is whose it feels like. Merchants who want
# a true custom domain already have that path (docs/runbooks/custom-domains.md);
# this is the good default for everyone who does not.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the app already does (app/main.py)
#
# `_store_for_host` resolves a Host header two ways: a VERIFIED custom domain, then
# a single-label platform subdomain via `_platform_subdomain_slug`. The custom
# domain is checked FIRST so this cannot shadow one.
#
# The subdomain path is deliberately strict, because Host is attacker-controlled:
#   * exactly one label  — `deep.nested.tilla.gudman.xyz` is refused
#   * must match SLUG_PATTERN — so `UPPER`, `bad_underscore`, `-leading` are refused
#   * must NOT be in RESERVED_SLUGS — so `api.tilla.gudman.xyz` can never serve a
#     storefront even if a row were somehow named `api`
#   * the store must be `status='live'` — a blocked or pending store stays dark
#   * the bare platform host resolves to no store, so it keeps serving the landing
#     page rather than becoming a shop
# All of those are covered by tests in tests/test_custom_domains.py.
#
# Note `custom_domain_root` builds its canonical and Open Graph URLs from the
# REQUEST host, not from `store.custom_domain` — a subdomain store has no
# custom_domain, and reading the column put the literal "None" into every URL on
# the page. That was a real bug caught in testing; do not "simplify" it back.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The vhost to add once DNS and the certificate exist
#
#     server {
#         listen 80; listen [::]:80;
#         server_name *.tilla.gudman.xyz;
#         location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ { root /var/www/html; }
#         location / { return 301 https://$host$request_uri; }
#     }
#     server {
#         listen 443 ssl http2; listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
#         server_name *.tilla.gudman.xyz;
#
#         ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/tilla-wildcard/fullchain.pem;
#         ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/tilla-wildcard/privkey.pem;
#
#         # Same six headers as the platform vhost. Restate them in full in any
#         # location that adds one of its own — a single add_header inside a
#         # location cancels EVERY inherited one (see nginx-hardening.snippet).
#         add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always;
#         add_header Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin always;
#         add_header X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN always;
#         add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000" always;
#         add_header Content-Security-Policy "frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; form-action 'self'" always;
#         add_header Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()" always;
#
#         location / {
#             limit_req zone=tilla_api burst=30 nodelay;
#             client_max_body_size 26m;
#             proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8040;
#             proxy_set_header Host $host;              # REQUIRED: the app resolves
#             proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;  # the store from Host
#             proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
#             proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
#         }
#     }
#
# `proxy_set_header Host $host` is load-bearing, not boilerplate: the app has no
# other way to know which store was asked for. Drop it and every subdomain 404s.
#
# IMPORTANT ordering note: the existing `server_name tilla.gudman.xyz` block is an
# EXACT name and nginx prefers exact names over wildcards, so adding this cannot
# steal traffic from the platform host. Keep the vhost file OUT of a `.bak` name
# inside sites-enabled — that directory is globbed unfiltered.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is blocked, and on whom
#
# 1. DNS — a wildcard A record on gudman.xyz:
#        *.tilla   A   75.119.153.252
#    Owner action, at the registrar (Namecheap; authoritative NS are
#    registrar-servers.com per docs/runbooks/custom-domains.md).
#
# 2. Certificate — a wildcard cert REQUIRES the DNS-01 challenge; the webroot
#    challenge every other Tilla cert uses cannot prove `*.`:
#        certbot certonly --cert-name tilla-wildcard \
#            --preferred-challenges dns \
#            -d '*.tilla.gudman.xyz' -d tilla.gudman.xyz
#    Manual DNS-01 means pasting a TXT record per issuance and again at every
#    renewal, which is a standing chore and will eventually be missed. Prefer the
#    Namecheap DNS API with a certbot DNS plugin so renewal stays unattended, and
#    only then raise this vhost's HSTS to a year — the same discipline applied in
#    nginx-hardening.snippet: prove renewal, THEN commit browsers to HTTPS.
#
# Do not add the vhost before both exist. nginx would answer for `*.` with the
# platform certificate and every store subdomain would greet visitors with a
# certificate-name mismatch.
